00:00:07.269 --> 00:00:09.190 align:start position:0%
on wednesday evening north korea  launched two ballistic missiles off its  east coast for the first time since  march this year  north korea state media released photos  and videos of the launch and it was  conducted from a railway in north  korea's central region  state media referred to them as  railway-born missiles  we see in the footage that a train  slowly comes out of the tunnel we see  some people in hazmat suits that come  out preparing for the launch the anchor  in the video says that the launch  occurred at dawn on september 15th  and this was the first time that north  korea was showing off its railway-borne  missile technology  the missiles flew about 500 miles at an  altitude of 37 miles before splashing  into the waters between korea and japan  which has caused alarm for officials  what's unique is that north korea has an  extensive railway network and this means  south korean and u.s officials now have  to track rail launch missiles as well as  road launchers and submarine launched  missiles  north korea has kept quiet for quite a  long time since the pandemic began north  korea has been consumed by domestic  problems it's kept its borders closed  largely due to covet fears and has  admitted to food shortages  a lot of north korea watchers say that  this recent missile launch indicates  that north korea wants to not only ramp  up pressure but also get the u.s to pay  attention to them and resume dialogue  weapons tests are definitely one of the  methods that north korea uses to dial up  pressure on washington  many experts that i've spoken to have  said  that because the us has been so consumed  with afghanistan and other international  matters this was sort of a chance for  north korea to grab their attention and  say you know we're still here we still  have our weapons and we still have this  military power to show off and they're  basically hoping to use that as leverage  once negotiations do eventually resume
 

